01:08:19,010 --> 01:14:44,550
When these children had left the house, from this it seems the children had been bathed. It was a very civil community. The mothers had quickly had them bathed, and had ornamented the children. All the children. With what. Glass, gunja seeds, jewels, and gold. The ornament had happened at home. Garlands of gunja, and jewels, and gold. With these all the children had been ornamented at home. Ornamented. And still they ornamented themselves. In the forest they began to make their own ornaments. With fruits, new leaves, clusters, flowers, peacock feathers, and colored earth. With fruits, and with the soft leaves of the trees, and with clusters. The flower and the leaf on whatever twig, on whatever branch, and if the flower is large, many together, and even one or two leaves, then that is a cluster. Sumana, with flowers. And piccha, with a feather. And with minerals. The red clay that is in the forest, with that. Rubbing it on a stone, they began to make pictures. On the cheek, on one another's forehead, they are ornamenting. They were ornamented. And they themselves ornamented. They have left the house ornamented with gold and diamond, and coming here, ornament yourselves with fruit, leaf, and clay. One acharya writes, in this connection, those who are great do not see a difference between clay and gold. When in the eye of the ordinary devotees of Lord Krishna there is no difference between clay and gold, then how will this difference be in the friends of the Lord. One whose ornament was done with gold, come and put on clay and ornament yourself. Make a good ornament of the red mineral. In this one verse is the description of the ornament. So this should be understood, the children first of all begin to ornament Krishna. This is not looking good. Krishna is in gold and silver, so they bring fruit, pick it, and bind it. Somewhere they hang the fruits at the throat. So Surdas has written this, that when the Lord would come to Braj in the evening, Mother Yashoda first of all would take off all these extra fruits and flowers, would throw them. And wetting a cloth. Who put on all this clay, this mineral. She would ask, what have you become and come. Mother did not like it. So wealthy a son, and he goes and puts on clay, and comes with a leaf bound here and there. Gold had been put on him. He had gone and come like this in Vrindavan. Now she would take it off and keep it. And whatever the leaf was, she would go on throwing it in the rubbish. She would throw it like something extra. The mother takes the ornament from the limbs. [Surdas] This is the first line of the pad, that she would take this ornament from the limbs of her own Krishna. So, although ornamented, they ornamented themselves. They were ornamented by the mothers, and still ornamented. On this one acharya makes one feeling. If a person has received some wealth from his parents, or a quality of a high family has been received, he should not become satisfied with that alone, that our parents are so great, therefore I am great. He should acquire his own quality. The inheritance of the parents is there, so it is there. This he should make his own. So these children are applying their own intelligence here. They are not satisfied with the ornament the parents made. They will make their own.

