01:14:45,410 --> 01:18:36,980
Some people say, our father was so great, so what of it. One says now, however great the father may be, the child becomes great by his own karma. Like Agastya. Agastya drank the ocean. And he is the son of a pot, the son of a kumbha. They are very great, so we also became great. Greatness happens in that direction, and that is not everything. These small children were not satisfied with the ornament done by their parents. At night they would wear all the ornaments, and to make themselves beautiful, or to feel better by their own understanding, they are ornamenting themselves with all these fruits and flowers of the forest. A mature man will hardly do this, that a gold ornament has been put on and in its place he puts on clay. He will not do it. Those children were doing it. And by this it was proved that to the Lord, and to those children of the Lord, to the cowherd friends, these ornaments are not dear. The Lord would ornament him, and all the children. Seven things, this is written. With seven articles all the children would make their own ornament. Although they stayed ornamented with glass, gunja, jewels, and gold, still they made their own ornament. On reaching the forest, the first lila they would do. The calf is grazing, attention does not stay on that. They began to ornament. Krishna. The coming and the being became the ornament here. Wherever they would arrive, they would take off that first ornament and say, this is good. Sometimes they would secretly take one another's basket. Let me take the article. Whoever is playing, it is kept somewhere, or it is hung, they would secretly take his basket. And that child would later give attention, who has taken my article. They would take it only in order to give it, and they would bewilder him. Who took it. This one took it. I will abuse you. I will do this. Tell me. It does not go on. So every day all this would go on. Who took ours. This was our article. This was for eating. Otherwise all of it has been brought only for Krishna. In truth there was great affection for one another. And still, in the play, it would happen like this. The day of the little theft. Someone stole someone's little box, or the stick, or the straw. He would steal the reed, and the horn. And when the knowledge came, when it was found that so-and-so has taken it, he would throw the basket to another. It would be found that it is with this one, so he would take it and give it to another. He would go there, and he would give it to a third. In this way they would bewilder him. And in this itself they would become greatly happy. And at the end, what he wanted, they would throw the article far, kal kal. Then they would give it, after the whole fuss. That complaining child would say, if you give it to me, I will give half the article from it. They did not have to take it. But laughing, they gave it again. Laughing, they would give it to him.

