If Krishna had gone far to see the beauty of the forest, I first, I first, touching him they enjoyed. If Krishna sometimes went far to see the beauty of the forest, the children would see him. Among the children there was a contest, who will touch Krishna first. I first, I first. I will touch first, I will touch first. And they would run. Going, they would embrace Krishna. I will touch first. And from these words this also is sounding, that Sri Krishna usually did not go far from the friends. He stayed with them always. Sometimes he would go out a little way, to see the beauty of the forest. Not very far. A little way. Then, not seeing Krishna, the children would become anxious. Or, to increase their anxiety, to increase their happiness, Krishna would sometimes be far. The word if is being put first. If he went far. Its meaning is, he did not go far. Very rarely was there an occasion when he went far. Then, to touch the Lord, to touch Krishna, a race would start among the children. Seeing him in front, they would run. I first, I first. I will touch, I will touch. They would run. And Krishna, to keep their pleasure, as many children as there were, he would become that many forms and meet them. And a child would say, I have touched first. He would say, I have touched first. They were playing. They were celebrating joy. Having touched. Having touched properly. Having touched the Lord properly, meaning having taken him in the arms. Tell me, Krishna, did I touch you first or not. Then everywhere Krishna would say, I was touched first. A race like this should be among devotees. First I will reach the Lord's morning arati. I will reach. I will do the worship. I will do the ornamenting. Here everyone steps back. This race should happen. In this way this is a divine contest. In truth one should stay ahead in worship. In laziness, and other people behind in talk. I first, I first. I first. I first, not you, not you. Here there is no not you, not you. But when the demons snatched the pot of nectar and ran, from the hand of Lord Dhanvantari, then for drinking they speak exactly this word. I first, I first. Not you, not you. Not you. A quarrel among them. A quarrel is happening between father and son. A quarrel is going on among relatives also. So contest, rivalry, this is natural. In the spiritual world also there is rivalry, there is contest. And it is centered on Krishna. First I will touch Krishna. Saying this, they would run. What a great fortune those children had. And to give them happiness in this way, Sri Krishna would sometimes be far. Walking together like this, will they embrace Krishna. In that so much joy does not come. When there is hunger. I will touch first, I will touch first. And all stayed first. No one behind. This is the Lord's being the Lord. From time to time he would manifest his own opulence. As many children as there were, becoming that many he would play. And he would show himself to only one child. They ran to touch Krishna. And one who will not be able to touch, he will be disappointed. So the Lord would appear near him. Or this feeling also is there, that by turns all received the embrace. Having touched. The Lord who is called unseeable. I will touch first. In this way they race. So this Vrindavan dhama is in truth the Lord's house. Here is his residence. Here there is no need to search for the Lord. To see the Lord, and to sing, to feed, to swing him. This alone is the work here. Outside Vrindavan one will still have to search for the Lord, whether he is or is not, where he went. Here there is no searching. This is to be seen tomorrow, and to be filled into a newspaper. A very great saint had said this thing, in a katha, that in Vrindavan Krishna is not to be searched for. In this way, the touch. They would touch Krishna properly. And they enjoyed. They played. They stayed in joy. Remire means they attained supreme joy. And the word remire is also telling this, that in this act the children had the greatest absorption.

