Some sounding flutes, some blowing horns. Some singing with the bees, and others cooing with the cuckoos. There were thousands and thousands of children. So some would play the flute. And many flutes together. Some children would keep playing the flute together somewhere. Then some would blow the horn. Some would keep singing with the bees. The bees that sing bhan-bhan on the flowers, with them they would match note to note and sing. And some would keep imitating the call of the cuckoo. They would keep saying kuhu-kuhu behind them. Grazing the calf was a work of the name alone. The real work was this. Playing. Sounding the flute, blowing the horn, singing with the bees, singing with the cuckoos. And they would run with the shadow of a bird. In the sky the birds would fly like that, spreading the wings, here and there. Then the children would run with their shadow. Some have this game. The shadow of a bird is falling from above, and they would run at the same speed. From here to there. The birds also, on purpose, would fly where the open ground was. They would move according to the bird's shadow. From here to there, like that. We also have seen, children move in this way, in yesterday's shadow of a bird. And some people would walk with a beautiful gait with the swan. On the earth also, and in the water also. On the ground and in the water, they would walk with the swan. Or the swan is swimming in the water, and these would walk like a swan on the land itself. And here and there the cranes would sit in meditation. Then these also would sit beside them, closing the eyes. They would meditate like the crane. The crane's meditation is very famous. A student should keep a meditation like the crane. In study. There were many boys. Not one or two. So some hundreds of boys would sit with the crane. Sitting near. Upa means near. Sitting means to sit, to enter. Like the crane. Some people would keep dancing with the peacocks. This, at the same time. All of this is happening together. And sometimes they would catch the tail of the young monkeys and pull. Then those young monkeys would begin to cry. They make a sound like that. And some children, holding their tail, not very hard, holding the tail, would climb the tree with them. From the Lord's time itself, monkeys live in Vrindavan. It is no matter of wonder. But at that time they did not take spectacles. It was so full of fruit and flower that those poor ones do not take the articles of others. Now people have articles filled in their houses, they do not give, so they snatch. And some people are such, in truth, that until something of theirs is snatched, they do not give a banana. To the monkeys. They are that miserly. On coming to Vrindavan, one should come having planned first. For the elder residents, for the monkeys, for the pigeon, for the peacocks. This sitting stops here.
